Practical No. 8
Pagination and Sorting with Spring Data JPA — Spring Boot (Maven) + H2 Database
Textbook Reference: Chapter 5 — Working with Spring Data JPA
Objective: Implement server-side pagination and sorting over a large result set using Spring Data's Pageable / PageRequest
abstraction (the PagingAndSortingRepository capability inherited by JpaRepository ), exposed through query parameters on a REST
endpoint.
Stack: Java 17, Spring Boot 3.3.4, Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, H2 (in-memory), Maven
1. Theory in Brief
Why pagination? Returning thousands of rows in one response wastes memory and bandwidth. Server-side pagination sends
only one slice (page) at a time, and the database does the limiting with LIMIT ... OFFSET ... .
Pageable — an interface describing which slice is wanted (page number, page size, sort order).
PageRequest.of(page, size, sort) — the standard implementation used to build a Pageable .
Sort.by(sortBy) — builds the ORDER BY clause; .ascending() / .descending() set the direction.
Page<T> — the result: the slice itself plus metadata ( totalElements , totalPages , number , size , first , last ). Spring
converts it to JSON automatically, so the method just returns it.
Page numbering is zero-based: page=0 is the first page.
