12. How to Run
1. Create the folder structure shown in section 2 and copy each file into place.
2. From the project root run mvn clean spring-boot:run (or run the main class from the IDE).
3. Wait for Tomcat started on port 8080 in the console.
4. Open http://localhost:8080/h2-console (JDBC URL jdbc:h2:mem:productdb , user sa , blank password) and run SELECT * FROM
PRODUCTS; to confirm the 20 rows loaded.
5. Fire the requests from section 10 in Postman.
13. Observations
With 20 records and size=5 , totalPages = 4 and totalElements = 20.
After the POST, totalElements becomes 21 and totalPages becomes 5 ( totalPages = ceil(totalElements / size) ).
After the PUT lowering the price to 8999, the record shifts to an earlier position when sorted by price.
After the DELETE, totalElements drops back by one and the last page shrinks.
Requesting a page beyond the last one returns 200 OK with an empty content[] , not an error.
Console SQL confirms Hibernate issues one LIMIT/OFFSET query for the slice plus one COUNT(*) query for totalElements .
14. Conclusion
Server-side pagination and sorting were implemented with Spring Data JPA. By accepting page , size and sortBy as request
parameters, building a Pageable with PageRequest.of(...) and returning Page<Product> from findAll(Pageable) , the API returns
only the requested slice together with the paging metadata, and the metadata stays consistent across create, update and delete
operations.
